      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2022-09-13T13:53:31.891Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "62df6580b1e50b465a4ada4e",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": {
        "like": 1
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "For folks wondering why this provides a boost, the reason is that this increases something called \"cache coherency\". CPUs have their own memory that is \"close\" to them. When a CPU needs access to something in memory, that data is copied into the CPU cache. Because the cache is closer and faster than normal \"main memory\" ram, it can increase the performance of future work it does that needs that data.\nThis fast cache memory is limited though, and if too many new things are loaded in (say by another program that runs on that CPU) then the data gets removed from the cache. The next time vrchat tries to access that data it will have to load it into the cache again.\nThe other issue with cache is that if vrchat switches to run on another CPU then that other CPU's cache won't have the data loaded into its cache which is slower.\nCPU affinity keeps vrchat running on the same set of CPU(s) which means that it is working with the same cached data. It also will tell the OS to try to push other programs to run on other CPU(s) which will help keep the data on that cache. \nThis is one of the reasons that consoles are so fast. CPU affinity and the lack of other programs running that can mess up a CPU cache help keep data that is needed on the fast memory in caches.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
